notes.body:
We've been using this build in the office for the last couple of weeks. Feedback was engineers wanted an option other than using the diagonal color divided style, the 85% was good on large screens, but on smaller screens a different percentage would be better, and that they wanted to be able to adjust coloring rule settings from the right click on a coloring rule in the frame. 

first I added a way to select what percentage of offset configurable from 75% to 95%. You'll also see in the layout that a separator style is now settable.


![image](/uploads/329ac7313284cc8be3e997b8abd51752/image.png){width=574 height=600}

Here's what those different styles look like.
![image](/uploads/d1937364513cdb25fc3277dc45b8bd68/image.png){width=900 height=135}

![image](/uploads/8f8f455a23848be29b288d384a8a5c81/image.png){width=900 height=133}

I also added shortcuts on the menu for coloring rule name/string

![image](/uploads/a456878505bff01e614c11f38ebad5b3/image.png){width=484 height=540}

Thank you Stig for taking the time to look at this MR.

If I'm following your question correctly, would the suggestion be to add to the "Style:" portion as coloring rule color separation option in that dialog, or add a new row? Something along those lines..

![image](/uploads/0cb765d158846661591739173861a1a8/image.png){width=900 height=543}

This will leak whenever a file is closed, because nothing frees the matches then.

There are two options:

1. Have `color_filter_colorize_packet_all` return a `wmem_list` which is in file scope, call wmem_destroy_list where you have `g_slist_free` and convert some operations to use wmem_list instead of GSList.
2. Using a wmem user callback, which does require unregistering the callback if you free the list when the proto data is replaced to avoid a double-free. (If you wait until the file is closed, it uses more memory while the file is open if the color filters are changed, but will free them all when it's closed.)
```
static bool
color_matches_free_cb(wmem_allocator_t *scope _U_, wmem_cb_event_t event _U_, void* data)
{
        GSList *matches = (GSList*)data;

        g_slist_free(matches);
        return false;
}
...
                        unsigned cb_id;
                        GSList *old_matches = (GSList *)p_get_proto_data(wmem_file_scope(), pinfo, proto_frame, 0);
                        if (old_matches) {
                                cb_id = GPOINTER_TO_UINT(p_get_proto_data(wmem_file_scope(), pinfo, proto_frame, 1));
                                wmem_unregister_callback(wmem_file_scope(), cb_id);
                                g_slist_free(old_matches);
                                p_remove_proto_data(wmem_file_scope(), pinfo, proto_frame, 0);
                        }
                        p_add_proto_data(wmem_file_scope(), pinfo, proto_frame, 0, matches);
                        cb_id = wmem_register_callback(wmem_file_scope(), color_matches_free_cb, matches);
                        p_add_proto_data(wmem_file_scope(), pinfo, proto_frame, 1, GUINT_TO_POINTER(cb_id));
```
That's a lot of callbacks, so instead each GSList could be added to a module internal map or something and there be one callback that frees them all (again, removing them from the map if they're freed manually.)

